= Abductive reasoning — Technical =

== IBE structure ==
Inference to the best explanation can be represented schematically:

<pre>
candidate set H = {H₁,...,Hₙ}
evidence E
score explanatory adequacy of Hᵢ for E
select/support comparatively best Hᵢ
</pre>

The critical questions are how the candidate set is generated and how explanatory adequacy is measured.

== Underdetermination ==
Several incompatible hypotheses can explain the same evidence.

An abductive conclusion is therefore sensitive to missing alternatives.

== Bayesian comparison ==
For candidate models:

<pre>
P(Hᵢ | E) ∝ P(E | Hᵢ) P(Hᵢ)
</pre>

Bayesian comparison can encode simplicity and fit probabilistically, but explanatory virtues do not map uniquely to priors or likelihoods.

== Normative status ==
Abduction is widely used but philosophically contested as an independent rule of rational inference.

Its reliability depends on candidate generation, explanatory criteria, and the connection between explanation and truth.

